## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about multi-word bit manipulations and safe 64→128-bit extension without loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit reversal via two 64-bit reversals and swapping halves
- Parallel-bits reversal (mask/shifts)
- Endianness-insensitive mapping of bit indices

## Code Example
```javascript
function reverse64(x) {
  x = (x >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n | (x & 0x5555555555555555n) << 1n;
  x = (x >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n | (x & 0x3333333333333333n) << 2n;
  x = (x >> 4n) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) << 4n;
  x = (x >> 8n) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) << 8n;
  x = (x >> 16n) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) << 16n;
  x = (x >> 32n) | (x << 32n);
  return x;
}
function reverse128(hi, lo) {
  const revLo = reverse64(lo);
  const revHi = reverse64(hi);
  return { hi: revLo, lo: revHi };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to streaming 128-bit values?
- How to verify correctness with random tests?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about cross-boundary 128-bit rotations without a native 128-bit type. Exercises correct handling of edge cases like r=0, r=64, and r=127, plus understanding how two 64-bit halves interact during rotation.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit numbers split into hi/lo halves
- Boundary-safe shifts across 64-bit parts
- Distinguishing r<64 vs r>=64 paths and their swap behavior

## Code Example

```c
uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned r) {
  r &= 127;
  if (r == 0) return x;
  if (r < 64) {
    uint64_t hi = (x.hi << r) | (x.lo >> (64 - r));
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo << r) | (x.hi >> (64 - r));
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  } else {
    unsigned t = r - 64;
    uint64_t hi = (x.lo << t) | (x.hi >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo >> (64 - t)) | (x.hi << t);
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement rotateRight128? 
- How do you unit-test edge cases (0, 64, 127) efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft compact, branchless bit-twiddling across a 128-bit type.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit split into hi/lo; XOR fold reduces width.
- Branchless reduction using successive shifts and XORs.
- Final parity is the low bit after folding.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Implementation in C-like syntax for readability
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
unsigned int parity128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t t = x.hi ^ x.lo;
  t ^= t >> 32;
  t ^= t >> 16;
  t ^= t >> 8;
  t ^= t >> 4;
  t ^= t >> 2;
  t ^= t >> 1;
  return (unsigned int)(t & 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you compute the full popcount for 128-bit without branches?
- How does this approach fare on different compilers and architectures?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to reason about cross-half bit patterns and leverage a reversal primitive to detect palindromicity without loops.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit representations split across two 64-bit halves
- Cross-half bit equality, palindrome concept
- Using an existing reversal primitive to avoid per-bit work

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch
int isPalindrome128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  uint64_t hi_rev, lo_rev;
  reverse128(hi, lo, &hi_rev, &lo_rev);
  return (hi == hi_rev && lo == lo_rev) ? 1 : 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- Could you implement a pure bitwise variant that does not rely on reverse128?
- How would you generalize to 256-bit values with four halves?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of branchless, scalable bit-twiddling techniques for 64-bit inputs.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR style bit reversal
- Masking and shifting sequences
- Branchless algorithms for performance

## Code Example
```c
#include <stdint.h>

uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t n) {
  n = ((n >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL) | ((n & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1);
  n = ((n >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL) | ((n & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2);
  n = ((n >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) | ((n & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4);
  n = ((n >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) | ((n & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8);
  n = ((n >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) | ((n & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16);
  n = (n >> 32) | (n << 32);
  return n;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for smaller word sizes or specialized ISAs?
- How would you adapt for 128-bit inputs and performance considerations?

## Why This Is Asked
