## Why This Is Asked
Tests per-byte parallel parity computation with pure bitwise ops.\n\n## Key Concepts
- 128-bit lane handling via two 64-bit halves\n- In-place parity reduction using shifts\n- Bitwise masking to extract per-byte results\n\n## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
uint128 perByteParity128(uint128 x){
  uint64_t lo = x.lo, hi = x.hi;
  lo ^= lo >> 4; lo ^= lo >> 2; lo ^= lo >> 1;
  hi ^= hi >> 4; hi ^= hi >> 2; hi ^= hi >> 1;
  uint64_t mask = 0x0101010101010101ULL;
  return (uint128){ .lo = lo & mask, .hi = hi & mask };
}
```
## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for non-byte-aligned lanes?\n- How would you verify correctness with minimal tests?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128-bit data as four independent 32-bit lanes and perform lane-wise rotation without loops. Demonstrates precise bit-slicing, correct boundary handling, and minimal, provable code paths.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit packing into 4×32 lanes
- Lane-wise rotation using (x<<n) | (x>>(32-n))
- Bit masking to confine intermediate results to 32-bit lanes
- Clamping n with & 31 to handle n >= 32

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64 lo; uint64 hi; } uint128;

uint128 rotateWithinLanes128(uint128 x, unsigned n) {
    n &= 31;
    uint32 a = (uint32)(x.lo);
    uint32 b = (uint32)(x.lo >> 32);
    uint32 c = (uint32)(x.hi);
    uint32 d = (uint32)(x.hi >> 32);

    x.lo = ((uint64)((a << n) | (a >> (32 - n))) << 32) |
           ((b << n) | (b >> (32 - n)));
    x.hi = ((uint64)((c << n) | (c >> (32 - n))) << 32) |
           ((d << n) | (d >> (32 - n)));
    return x;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests endianness understanding and safe 128-bit lane manipulation with pure bitwise ops.

## Key Concepts
- Byte reversal with masks
- 128-bit struct arithmetic
- Endianness swapping

## Code Example
```javascript
static inline uint64_t reverseBytes64(uint64_t x){
  x = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
  x = ((x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
  x = (x << 32) | (x >> 32);
  return x;
}

uint128 swapEndian128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = reverseBytes64(x.lo);
  r.lo = reverseBytes64(x.hi);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for a compile-time constant shift? 
- How to verify with unit tests across edge cases (all zeros, all ones, cross-boundary values)?
